# the argument of hume-treatise-1.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Hume. "Must" in S1 is read as a practical requirement: to begin regularly, i.e. to reason justly
# about causation, we have to trace the idea to its origin. It is written as a conditional, not a modal.
# The "impossible ... without" claims in S2 are read as plain conditionals, without modal operators.
atom	reason_justly	we reason justly (begin regularly) concerning causation
atom	understand_idea	we understand perfectly the idea of causation
atom	trace_origin	we trace the idea of causation up to its origin and examine the primary impression from which it arises
atom	clear_idea	the idea of causation has clearness
atom	clear_reasoning	our reasoning concerning causation has a like clearness
P1	S2	reason_justly → understand_idea	kind conceptual
P2	S2	understand_idea → trace_origin	kind conceptual
P3	S3	trace_origin → clear_idea
P4	S3	clear_idea → clear_reasoning
C	S1	reason_justly → trace_origin
